1. ps aux
     lists running programs
   | passes that output on as the input of
2. grep -i nginx
     keeps lines matching /nginx/, ignoring case
     /nginx/ means: "nginx"
   | passes that output on as the input of
3. awk "{print \$2}"
     prints column 2
   | passes that output on as the input of
4. sort -n
     sorts the lines as numbers
   | passes that output on as the input of
5. head -3
     keeps the first 3 lines
     >> pids.txt: adds the output to the end of pids.txt
     2>&1: error messages go wherever the output goes
result: added to the end of pids.txt (nothing on screen)
1. grep -E '^[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{4}$' phones.txt
     keeps lines matching /^[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{4}$/ in phones.txt
     /^[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{4}$/ means: the line starts, then exactly 3 digits, then "-", then exactly 4 digits, then the line ends
result: the matching lines
1. sed -E 's/([a-z]+)@old\.com/\1@new.org/g' users.txt
     replaces /([a-z]+)@old\.com/ with "\1@new.org" (every match on each line) in users.txt
     /([a-z]+)@old\.com/ means: (one or more lowercase letters), then "@old.com"
     > out.txt: saves the output in out.txt, replacing what was there
   && if that worked, then
2. wc -l out.txt
     counts the lines in out.txt
result: depends on which commands run (&& and || decide)
1. cat
     prints what it reads
     << EOF: the lines you type next, up to EOF, become the input (a here-document)
   | passes that output on as the input of
2. grep -v "^#"
     keeps lines NOT matching /^#/
     /^#/ means: the line starts, then "#"
result: the lines that don't match
